WEBVTT

00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:19.890
Leave it to Beaver, starring Barbara Billingsley, Hugh Beaumont, Tony Dow, and Jerry Mathers as the Beaver.

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:25.920
To a boy, his father often seemed stern and forbidding.

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:29.960
He might even be unfair enough to make you rake the lawn when you wanted to play.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:35.180
But once in a while, you're surprised to learn he's actually on your side.

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:38.520
And that's our story tonight on Leave it to Beaver.

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:47.930
What's your husband?

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:50.670
What's for supper?

00:00:51.670 --> 00:00:54.810
Steak and mushrooms, corn on the cob, lima beans, and Waldorf salad.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:01.980
All right, dear, what did you buy, break, or lose?

00:01:03.390 --> 00:01:05.970
I was going to tell you over the Boston cream pie,

00:01:06.110 --> 00:01:08.450
but seeing as my elaborate menu has given me away,

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:11.240
I have to leave the first thing Saturday morning.

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:11.920
Huh?

00:01:12.620 --> 00:01:13.740
Peggy had her baby.

00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:14.940
Oh, boy?

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:15.680
Girl.

00:01:16.070 --> 00:01:16.330
Oh.

00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:19.140
I promised I'd go up for a few days and help her out.

00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:22.240
Well, that's fine for Peggy's baby,

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:23.900
but what about the two babies you're leaving here?

00:01:24.670 --> 00:01:26.610
Three babies I'm leaving here.

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:29.860
Oh, darling, you'll be all right.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:31.170
Sure, we will.

00:01:31.930 --> 00:01:34.550
Aunt Martha's gonna come and stay with you till I get back.

00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:36.760
Aunt Martha?

00:01:38.050 --> 00:01:40.350
Now, what's the matter with my Aunt Martha?

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:44.240
Well, nothing that you can put your finger on.

00:01:44.890 --> 00:01:46.550
It's just that whenever I'm in the room with her,

00:01:46.610 --> 00:01:48.610
I feel like I should be apologizing for something.

00:01:49.370 --> 00:01:50.840
Hey, look, I got a great idea.

00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:53.500
Why doesn't Aunt Martha go and help Peggy and you stay here?

00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:55.320
Aunt Martha's never been married.

00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:58.080
She doesn't know anything about babies and things.

00:01:59.180 --> 00:01:59.960
Someone must have...

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:02.340
I explained a few things to her somewhere along the line.

00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:06.980
Gee, Mom, the pie was good, but did you have to go away?

00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:10.240
Yes, dear, I'm afraid I do. You see, my sister Peggy had a baby.

00:02:10.760 --> 00:02:12.100
Isn't that a wonderful surprise?

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:15.720
But, Mom, if you're gonna be gone, who's gonna take care of me and the beaver?

00:02:16.150 --> 00:02:18.090
Your Aunt Martha's gonna come and stay with you.

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:22.580
Aunt Martha, is she the one with the husband we're not supposed to talk about?

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:26.820
That's one of your father's relatives.

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:29.980
Is that more?

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:31.760
That one that sends a soap every Christmas?

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:35.710
Oh, no, Aunt Martha understands boys better than that.

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:37.040
She sends umbrellas.

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:41.080
Oh, yeah, she's our umbrella aunt.

00:02:42.460 --> 00:02:45.380
I remember her. She has birds on her hat.

00:02:45.580 --> 00:02:46.080
That's right.

00:02:49.060 --> 00:02:53.320
Would you boys take your plates out in the kitchen, please?

00:02:53.860 --> 00:02:54.380
Okay, Mom.

00:02:58.620 --> 00:02:59.660
They're nice birds, though.

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:07.060
Ward, I didn't like that remark about the umbrellas.

00:03:07.300 --> 00:03:10.100
Now, when Aunt Martha's here, I don't want you undermining her.

00:03:11.210 --> 00:03:14.290
Dear, I've never been known to go around undermining old ladies.

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:18.760
Now, I know her ideas seem old-fashioned and trying at times,

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:19.940
but she's very sensitive.

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:22.880
And I want you to see that the boys obey her, they're polite,

00:03:22.940 --> 00:03:24.260
and they're on their best behavior.

00:03:24.970 --> 00:03:27.010
June, believe me, I understand.

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:29.260
I've got problem relatives, too.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:31.640
Now, wait a minute, sir.

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:34.580
Of course I'm not going to let the boys be rude to her.

00:03:34.850 --> 00:03:38.590
We'll do everything we can to make her stay as pleasant as possible.

00:03:41.710 --> 00:03:44.650
If you want me to, I'll even have the boys meet her at the airport carrying their umbrellas.

00:03:47.710 --> 00:03:57.900
I must say, Ward, you have a very nice place here.

00:03:58.340 --> 00:03:59.500
Very nice indeed.

00:04:01.630 --> 00:04:03.850
You must be doing rather well now.

00:04:06.860 --> 00:04:09.880
Well, we managed to stay one jump ahead of the sheriff.

00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:15.080
The boys should be home any minute now.

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:17.520
They're looking forward to seeing you again, Aunt Martha.

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:20.300
Oh, they must be quite the little gentlemen by now.

00:04:20.620 --> 00:04:24.250
I do hope they're getting good use out of their umbrellas.

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:26.160
Oh, indeed they are.

00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:28.300
They really look forward to the rainy season.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:32.520
I hope you're still considering an Eastern college.

00:04:34.410 --> 00:04:37.510
Well, we thought we'd think about that when the time comes.

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:41.280
There's a certain polish it gives a boy that, uh...

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:41.840
Hey, Martha!

00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:53.020
Uh, boys, uh, this is your Aunt Martha.

00:04:53.480 --> 00:04:55.220
You remember her, of course.

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:04.540
Aunt Martha, this is Wally.

00:05:05.130 --> 00:05:08.130
Wallace, I'd know you anywhere.

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:14.360
You certainly look like a Bronson.

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:16.540
Just like a Bronson.

00:05:17.300 --> 00:05:17.820
Yeah.

00:05:19.500 --> 00:05:21.300
And this is the beaver.

00:05:22.350 --> 00:05:25.580
Beaver? I thought your name was Theodore.

00:05:26.020 --> 00:05:29.390
Oh, I don't use it much. I didn't know how to pronounce it until last year.

00:05:31.530 --> 00:05:34.430
Uh, well, uh, boys, now that your Aunt Martha's here,

00:05:34.450 --> 00:05:35.930
suppose we take her bags up to her room, huh?

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:40.160
Okay, Dad. Um, nice to have met you, Aunt Martha.

00:05:40.360 --> 00:05:41.240
Thank you, Wallace.

00:05:44.130 --> 00:05:45.520
Oh, me too.

00:05:45.900 --> 00:05:46.680
All right.

00:05:52.180 --> 00:05:53.480
Hey, Dad, what's a bronzer?

00:05:53.660 --> 00:05:53.880
Shh.

00:06:02.010 --> 00:06:04.210
Well, Aunt Martha, would you like another cup of coffee?

00:06:04.930 --> 00:06:05.850
No, dear, no.

00:06:06.450 --> 00:06:07.490
No, thank you.

00:06:09.510 --> 00:06:11.810
Did you notice a big change in the boys?

00:06:12.340 --> 00:06:14.640
Oh, yes, they really have grown.

00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:19.980
I suppose the older boy has to wear those blue jeans.

00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:20.940
Teenagers.

00:06:21.910 --> 00:06:23.270
Nothing to be done about that now.

00:06:24.970 --> 00:06:28.470
But I must say, little Theodore was somewhat of a shock.

00:06:30.160 --> 00:06:33.980
Well, I've tried to get those particular trousers away from him several times,

00:06:34.040 --> 00:06:36.920
but he keeps getting them back out of the Goodwill box.

00:06:38.940 --> 00:06:44.450
June, while you're gone, I'd just love to take little Theodore shopping.

00:06:44.730 --> 00:06:47.410
I thought I could get him something more, um...

00:06:47.980 --> 00:06:51.220
Well, after all, he's a Bronson, too, you know.

00:06:52.100 --> 00:06:56.640
Oh, now, Aunt Martha, it isn't necessary to do anything like that.

00:06:56.900 --> 00:06:59.320
Well, if you think I'm interfering...

00:06:59.320 --> 00:06:59.640
Oh, no!

00:07:00.730 --> 00:07:02.090
I didn't mean that at all.

00:07:02.690 --> 00:07:04.790
It's really very sweet of you.

00:07:05.250 --> 00:07:13.690
Now, look, boys, I'll be home at the end of the week.

00:07:13.910 --> 00:07:15.410
Now, don't forget your baths and Beaver.

00:07:15.530 --> 00:07:17.810
I put four pair of clean socks in your top drawer,

00:07:17.830 --> 00:07:19.590
and I don't want to find them there when I get back.

00:07:19.770 --> 00:07:20.450
Oh, no, Mom.

00:07:20.590 --> 00:07:21.210
Hurry up, June.

00:07:21.310 --> 00:07:22.170
We haven't got much time.

00:07:22.290 --> 00:07:23.050
Just a minute, dear.

00:07:23.130 --> 00:07:24.390
I'm saying goodbye to the boys.

00:07:25.360 --> 00:07:26.950
Mom, you gotta go.

00:07:28.270 --> 00:07:29.850
Yes, Beaver, I'm afraid I do.

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:33.030
You see, I'm going to go and help your Aunt Peggy.

00:07:33.320 --> 00:07:35.300
But while I'm away, you boys can help me.

00:07:35.700 --> 00:07:36.240
How, Mom?

00:07:36.890 --> 00:07:39.810
Well, I want you to do everything Aunt Martha asks you to.

00:07:40.390 --> 00:07:43.110
Even if she gives us milk toast for breakfast again?

00:07:45.830 --> 00:07:50.460
Now, look, boys, sometimes older people have different ideas.

00:07:51.100 --> 00:07:53.020
You see, it's been a long time since they were young,

00:07:53.100 --> 00:07:55.640
and, well, they sort of forget what it was like.

00:07:57.120 --> 00:07:59.980
You just remember while I'm away that if you...

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:03.340
You make Aunt Martha happy, you make me happy.

00:08:03.880 --> 00:08:04.580
Okay, Mom.

00:08:06.160 --> 00:08:06.900
Okay, Mom.

00:08:07.080 --> 00:08:08.840
I'll make you happy, I'll make Aunt Martha happy,

00:08:08.900 --> 00:08:10.220
and I'll change my socks every day.

00:08:10.820 --> 00:08:14.380
Oh, look, boys, I have to go.

00:08:14.600 --> 00:08:15.480
Goodbye, Aunt Martha.

00:08:15.920 --> 00:08:16.860
Now, remember...

00:08:16.860 --> 00:08:17.580
Goodbye, dear.

00:08:17.860 --> 00:08:17.940
Goodbye, Mom.

00:08:18.080 --> 00:08:20.060
Now, don't worry about a thing.

00:08:22.910 --> 00:08:25.530
Well, Theodore, right after lunch,

00:08:25.650 --> 00:08:28.790
you and I are going on a little shopping tour.

00:08:32.520 --> 00:08:34.380
Oh, boy, well, you think I might get it?

00:08:34.660 --> 00:08:35.260
Get what?

00:08:35.580 --> 00:08:37.760
The black leather jack with the eagle on the back.

00:08:55.570 --> 00:08:58.170
That's what I call a real little gentleman.

00:09:01.880 --> 00:09:07.800
and not many mothers nowadays have such excellent taste oh I'm not his mother

00:09:07.800 --> 00:09:18.860
uh-huh mother wouldn't have a baby with Aunt Peggy exquisite material English

00:09:18.860 --> 00:09:25.970
isn't it naturally madam these are cold why when my brothers were your age they

00:09:25.970 --> 00:09:29.310
wore trousers like that winter and summer shall I send these madam

00:09:30.030 --> 00:09:33.700
I think not. You may, uh, dispose of them.

00:09:35.140 --> 00:09:36.240
I'd be delighted to, madam.

00:09:36.850 --> 00:09:39.500
Wait a minute. I've got something in the pocket.

00:09:42.060 --> 00:09:43.180
Heavens, what is that?

00:09:43.800 --> 00:09:45.060
Just a dead goldfish.

00:09:46.460 --> 00:09:48.320
Me and Molly are gonna give it a funeral.

00:09:49.820 --> 00:09:50.400
Uh, yes.

00:09:51.220 --> 00:09:52.820
You may make out a bill for them.

00:09:53.060 --> 00:09:53.400
Yes, madam.

00:09:55.790 --> 00:09:59.580
Theodore, I think you look very nice.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:04.430
I feel funny. Almost like I have no clothes on at all.

00:10:05.310 --> 00:10:06.710
We mustn't be indelicate.

00:10:12.880 --> 00:10:14.160
We're going back in the bus, are we?

00:10:15.020 --> 00:10:18.310
No, we'd be late for supper. I think we'll take a taxi.

00:10:22.520 --> 00:10:23.380
Beaves, you're kidding.

00:10:24.060 --> 00:10:26.000
No, I'm not. I have Martha bought it for me.

00:10:26.940 --> 00:10:28.700
Boy, get a load of them me's.

00:10:33.420 --> 00:10:35.680
You look like a chicken with all his feathers off.

00:10:36.600 --> 00:10:38.840
Do I look that bad, Wally?

00:10:39.150 --> 00:10:40.170
Boy, I'll say.

00:10:40.870 --> 00:10:43.090
Lucky it's a new suit and you don't have to wear it to school.

00:10:43.600 --> 00:10:45.400
Kids didn't really give you the business.

00:10:46.240 --> 00:10:49.240
Yeah, lucky it's a new suit and I don't have to wear it to school, huh, Wally?

00:10:49.720 --> 00:10:51.000
Yeah, brother, I'll say.

00:10:51.470 --> 00:10:52.510
Get the biggest rep.

00:10:56.160 --> 00:10:58.780
Theodore, you must be sure and hang up your new clothes.

00:10:58.980 --> 00:10:59.960
We want them nice...

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:01.420
and fresh for school on Monday.

00:11:05.490 --> 00:11:06.810
Yes, Aunt Martha.

00:11:21.580 --> 00:11:24.220
Theodore, don't sump over your milk toast like that.

00:11:24.320 --> 00:11:25.800
You'll have curvature of the spine.

00:11:26.470 --> 00:11:28.810
But if I eat close to boy, I don't spill so much.

00:11:31.850 --> 00:11:34.310
Your Aunt Martha's right, Theodore.

00:11:35.050 --> 00:11:38.560
Why don't you just sort of meet the spoon halfway?

00:11:40.540 --> 00:11:41.360
Yes, Dad.

00:11:42.750 --> 00:11:44.590
Well, I have an early appointment.

00:11:44.690 --> 00:11:45.490
I've got to get going.

00:11:47.190 --> 00:11:49.310
Oh, excellent breakfast, Martha.

00:11:49.450 --> 00:11:49.870
Excellent.

00:11:50.390 --> 00:11:54.440
And you boys, you better hurry, too.

00:11:54.680 --> 00:11:57.600
I have your clothes all laid out in your room.

00:12:04.530 --> 00:12:07.240
Oh, I'm in an awful hurry, Theodore, I mean, uh, Beaver.

00:12:07.380 --> 00:12:08.720
Uh, is it really important?

00:12:09.180 --> 00:12:11.220
Well, kind of sorry. Do you see Aunt Martha bought me?

00:12:11.240 --> 00:12:13.560
Look, uh, Beaver, I'm sorry. I'm late now.

00:12:13.660 --> 00:12:15.600
I'll tell you what, you hang on to it until I get back tonight.

00:12:15.700 --> 00:12:17.160
Then we'll have lots of time to talk about it, okay?

00:12:18.810 --> 00:12:19.130
Okay.

00:12:19.430 --> 00:12:23.010
Did you get to ask him?

00:12:23.560 --> 00:12:23.880
No.

00:12:25.340 --> 00:12:27.580
Maybe I should tell Aunt Martha I can't win in school.

00:12:28.360 --> 00:12:29.360
He can't do that.

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:31.540
I promise Mom we wouldn't make any trouble.

00:12:32.710 --> 00:12:33.390
Oh, yeah.

00:12:34.880 --> 00:12:35.640
Well, don't worry, Pete.

00:12:36.100 --> 00:12:39.100
The kids will probably stop laughing at you after the first couple of classes.

00:12:43.510 --> 00:12:49.710
We got here early.

00:12:50.080 --> 00:12:51.920
Probably none of the other kids are here yet.

00:12:52.570 --> 00:12:54.590
Well, we'll just get to my desk.

00:12:55.100 --> 00:12:57.820
I'm gonna sit there, and I'm not gonna leave it for the whole day.

00:12:58.060 --> 00:12:58.460
Yeah?

00:12:59.120 --> 00:12:59.980
I'm not gonna get...

00:13:00.640 --> 00:13:01.520
Not for nothing.

00:13:09.620 --> 00:13:11.040
Maybe if you kind of crouch down,

00:13:11.120 --> 00:13:12.440
they won't look like short pants.

00:13:12.860 --> 00:13:16.760
Good luck anyway.

00:13:17.180 --> 00:13:18.280
I got to get to my class.

00:13:36.040 --> 00:13:37.900
Good morning, Theodore.

00:14:04.220 --> 00:14:10.240
Oh, my God.

00:14:31.000 --> 00:14:39.790
We're just getting a drink of water.

00:14:40.480 --> 00:14:43.920
Beaver, what happened to the bottoms of your pants?

00:14:45.480 --> 00:14:47.500
Where'd you get the girls' stocking?

00:14:47.960 --> 00:14:50.360
Is that a new kind of underwear, Beaver?

00:14:50.800 --> 00:14:52.920
It's a suit and it's supposed to be like this.

00:14:53.380 --> 00:14:55.520
Then why were you hiding all day?

00:14:56.100 --> 00:14:58.880
Hey, Beaver, you got dimples on your knees.

00:14:59.080 --> 00:14:59.960
I have not.

00:15:00.640 --> 00:15:01.840
You know something, Beaver?

00:15:01.940 --> 00:15:02.820
You're a sissy.

00:15:03.600 --> 00:15:04.520
Take it back.

00:15:05.040 --> 00:15:05.820
I will not.

00:15:05.960 --> 00:15:06.820
You're a sissy.

00:15:08.480 --> 00:15:10.380
Beaver, you leave Larry alone!

00:15:11.370 --> 00:15:24.210
Come on.

00:15:25.140 --> 00:15:25.600
Get out.

00:15:26.170 --> 00:15:27.390
On your feet, everybody.

00:15:33.320 --> 00:15:34.060
Now, what happened?

00:15:34.800 --> 00:15:36.120
Beaver hit Larry.

00:15:36.400 --> 00:15:37.710
Beaver hit everybody.

00:15:38.050 --> 00:15:38.430
Yeah!

00:15:39.090 --> 00:15:40.300
All right, all right.

00:15:40.920 --> 00:15:41.540
That'll be enough.

00:15:41.840 --> 00:15:42.180
Go on.

00:15:42.300 --> 00:15:42.780
On your way.

00:15:42.900 --> 00:15:43.300
Come on.

00:15:43.520 --> 00:15:58.320
Start this fight, Beaver.

00:15:59.200 --> 00:15:59.820
No, sir.

00:16:00.090 --> 00:16:01.050
My pants did.

00:16:06.500 --> 00:16:08.340
Well, I think you'd better run along home now.

00:16:11.620 --> 00:16:12.880
Yes, Mr. Boonegarten.

00:16:27.150 --> 00:16:29.070
Yeah, that's right, Mrs. Ward-Cleaver.

00:16:29.740 --> 00:16:29.960
All right.

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:30.320
Thank you.

00:16:32.100 --> 00:16:33.020
Glad you're home, Dad.

00:16:33.580 --> 00:16:34.260
Oh, hi, Wally.

00:16:34.610 --> 00:16:36.450
Just put in a long distance call to your mother.

00:16:36.590 --> 00:16:38.150
Kind of lonesome around here without her, isn't it?

00:16:38.680 --> 00:16:39.000
Yeah.

00:16:39.960 --> 00:16:41.800
Can I talk to you about something?

00:16:42.070 --> 00:16:42.330
Sure.

00:16:43.470 --> 00:16:46.600
There was a little trouble in school today.

00:16:47.820 --> 00:16:50.300
Wally, I told you last night, you have

00:16:50.300 --> 00:16:52.820
to invert those fractions before you divide.

00:16:53.110 --> 00:16:55.210
No, I did that, but they still came out wrong.

00:16:58.400 --> 00:16:59.680
This is about the beaver.

00:17:00.830 --> 00:17:02.290
He had a fight today in school.

00:17:02.640 --> 00:17:03.340
A fight?

00:17:04.180 --> 00:17:05.520
Well, that's a fine thing.

00:17:05.580 --> 00:17:08.120
Your mother's been gone two days, and he's in trouble already.

00:17:08.520 --> 00:17:10.100
Well, it wasn't actually his fault.

00:17:10.890 --> 00:17:11.870
Well, whose fault was it?

00:17:13.090 --> 00:17:14.390
It was Aunt Martha's.

00:17:14.820 --> 00:17:15.840
Aunt Martha's?

00:17:15.900 --> 00:17:18.940
Aunt Martha's?

00:17:19.450 --> 00:17:22.130
Yeah, she made the beaver wear those short pants to school.

00:17:22.370 --> 00:17:23.650
The kids picked on him.

00:17:24.600 --> 00:17:27.340
She made beaver wear short pants to school?

00:17:27.890 --> 00:17:28.950
You know how it is.

00:17:30.570 --> 00:17:30.990
Sure do.

00:17:31.090 --> 00:17:33.350
I had to wear long white stockings to school once.

00:17:35.190 --> 00:17:35.950
Where is he now?

00:17:36.400 --> 00:17:38.560
He's up in the room and he says he's not going to school

00:17:38.560 --> 00:17:38.960
tomorrow.

00:17:40.840 --> 00:17:42.260
Well, I'll tell you what you do, Wally.

00:17:42.810 --> 00:17:44.590
You go up there and tell him I'll take care of everything.

00:17:45.120 --> 00:17:46.680
Should be a load off his mind.

00:17:52.420 --> 00:17:54.100
Martha, Martha, can I speak to you a minute?

00:17:57.070 --> 00:17:57.710
Yes, Ward.

00:18:01.020 --> 00:18:01.580
Oh, uh.

00:18:01.720 --> 00:18:02.320
What is it?

00:18:02.590 --> 00:18:04.280
Well, uh, nothing, really.

00:18:05.000 --> 00:18:05.880
Won't you sit down?

00:18:06.670 --> 00:18:08.910
I really should take another peek at my eggplant.

00:18:09.880 --> 00:18:10.460
But, uh.

00:18:12.870 --> 00:18:15.810
Something has come up in connection with the boys, Martha.

00:18:15.810 --> 00:18:18.640
And I think we ought to straighten it out.

00:18:18.950 --> 00:18:19.930
Something I've done?

00:18:21.160 --> 00:18:24.460
Well, as a matter of fact, oh, excuse me.

00:18:24.520 --> 00:18:25.260
That must be June.

00:18:25.810 --> 00:18:26.250
Hello.

00:18:28.160 --> 00:18:29.160
Yes, this is Ward Cleaver.

00:18:31.150 --> 00:18:31.850
Oh, hello, June.

00:18:32.190 --> 00:18:33.810
Hello, Ward. How are you?

00:18:35.230 --> 00:18:36.650
Oh, everything's just fine here.

00:18:36.650 --> 00:18:38.430
I'm going to be home sooner than I expected.

00:18:38.530 --> 00:18:40.970
The day after tomorrow.

00:18:41.670 --> 00:18:42.730
How's everything at home?

00:18:43.220 --> 00:18:45.060
Oh, everything's fine here.

00:18:45.220 --> 00:18:46.880
Just fine. Couldn't be better.

00:18:48.230 --> 00:18:50.590
Oh, yes, yes. Aunt Martha's right here.

00:18:51.270 --> 00:18:53.770
Oh, she's been a tremendous help, June.

00:18:54.180 --> 00:18:55.920
Whipping up a wonderful dinner tonight.

00:18:56.200 --> 00:18:57.220
Eggplant and everything.

00:18:58.270 --> 00:18:59.070
Oh, eggplant?

00:19:01.050 --> 00:19:05.990
Well, Ward, I know that Aunt Martha can be a trial at times,

00:19:06.010 --> 00:19:08.540
but when I was a little girl, she was practically

00:19:08.540 --> 00:19:12.460
the only mother I knew, and I love her very dearly.

00:19:14.140 --> 00:19:16.780
You and the boys won't do anything to upset her,

00:19:16.860 --> 00:19:17.160
will you?

00:19:18.270 --> 00:19:20.030
Oh, no, no, of course not.

00:19:21.110 --> 00:19:23.730
Oh, as a matter of fact, we're getting along

00:19:23.730 --> 00:19:25.330
like four peas in a pod.

00:19:27.330 --> 00:19:29.970
Yeah, she's s**t.

00:19:32.520 --> 00:19:34.920
All right, dear. All right.

00:19:35.270 --> 00:19:37.030
Well, I'm glad everything's going well there.

00:19:37.190 --> 00:19:38.930
Yeah. And don't worry about us.

00:19:39.120 --> 00:19:41.020
Yeah. Well, good to hear your voice.

00:19:41.120 --> 00:19:42.510
We miss you. Bye.

00:19:46.820 --> 00:19:51.140
Well, everything's just fine up at Peggy's.

00:19:51.240 --> 00:19:52.520
I'm glad to hear that.

00:19:53.740 --> 00:19:55.180
Now, what was this you were saying?

00:19:55.960 --> 00:19:57.180
Something about the boys.

00:19:58.850 --> 00:19:59.970
Oh, uh... Oh, something...

00:20:00.820 --> 00:20:06.100
Well, now, isn't that silly?

00:20:06.220 --> 00:20:08.000
It's completely slipped my mind.

00:20:08.260 --> 00:20:09.620
I must be getting old.

00:20:11.670 --> 00:20:12.110
Yes.

00:20:14.520 --> 00:20:16.580
Well, I must get back to my eggplants.

00:20:25.700 --> 00:20:27.440
Yeah, Bede, you got nothing to worry about.

00:20:27.700 --> 00:20:29.780
Dad says he's gonna take care of the whole thing.

00:20:30.600 --> 00:20:31.860
Gee, thanks, Wally.

00:20:32.120 --> 00:20:35.140
I don't mind fighting with Larry and those guys,

00:20:35.500 --> 00:20:37.440
but that Judy, she scratches.

00:20:42.480 --> 00:20:44.100
I've sewed up the lining, Theodore.

00:20:44.520 --> 00:20:47.960
I hope we'll be more careful with our new clothes today.

00:20:48.520 --> 00:20:49.600
Yes, Aunt Martha.

00:20:51.820 --> 00:20:52.880
Wally, where's Dad?

00:20:53.410 --> 00:20:54.510
Gee, Beeb, I don't know.

00:20:54.820 --> 00:20:56.500
Well, your father left for the office.

00:20:57.800 --> 00:20:58.220
Left?

00:21:00.380 --> 00:21:02.100
Uh, well, I got an early class.

00:21:02.200 --> 00:21:03.040
I'd better get going.

00:21:11.790 --> 00:21:12.650
Bye, Aunt Martha.

00:21:15.900 --> 00:21:16.840
Good boy, Wallace.

00:21:17.300 --> 00:21:18.180
Yeah, sure.

00:21:26.710 --> 00:21:28.560
Martha, are you sure Dad left for work?

00:21:28.790 --> 00:21:29.690
Oh, yes, Dad.

00:21:32.260 --> 00:21:34.620
Yes, Theodore.

00:21:37.450 --> 00:21:38.170
Never mind.

00:21:38.790 --> 00:21:40.170
I'd better get to school, too.

00:21:50.020 --> 00:21:50.540
Theodore.

00:21:52.970 --> 00:21:54.050
Wallace forgot his.

00:21:54.680 --> 00:21:55.600
It looks like rain.

00:21:55.740 --> 00:21:57.080
I think you'd better take yours.

00:21:57.440 --> 00:21:58.480
Yes, Aunt Martha.

00:22:15.630 --> 00:22:23.430
Leave her out of those things.

00:22:23.830 --> 00:22:24.430
Let's lift these up.

00:22:29.570 --> 00:22:29.930
Cut it, cut.

00:22:30.160 --> 00:22:33.780
I wouldn't do a thing like that, but she's your mother's favorite aunt.

00:22:33.940 --> 00:22:36.240
We can't hurt her feelings, can we?

00:22:37.630 --> 00:22:38.710
Well, you don't have to do that.

00:22:39.090 --> 00:22:40.410
I think we can put these all right over.

00:22:41.450 --> 00:22:43.990
Okay, point your toe. Attaboy. Now the other one.

00:22:46.070 --> 00:22:51.050
You zip them up, I'll get you a jacket.

00:22:53.720 --> 00:22:55.280
Now, when you get home from school today,

00:22:55.830 --> 00:22:59.960
you can just slip back in here and change back again.

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:01.560
There you are.

00:23:02.170 --> 00:23:03.690
Ah, you look more like the old Beeb.

00:23:05.730 --> 00:23:06.830
Gee, thanks, Dad.

00:23:08.660 --> 00:23:09.860
You're almost like one of the fellas.

00:23:11.470 --> 00:23:13.690
Beaver, I think that's the nicest thing you ever said to me.

00:23:14.380 --> 00:23:15.280
Well, I'd better be going.

00:23:15.680 --> 00:23:16.180
Yeah, sure.

00:23:16.780 --> 00:23:17.340
Here's your books.

00:23:23.300 --> 00:23:28.930
Hey, Beaver, it looks a little like rain outside.

00:23:30.030 --> 00:23:31.210
I think I'd better take this.

00:23:32.080 --> 00:23:59.710
I just can't thank you enough for...

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:03.100
and visiting us and for watching over the boys while I was away.

00:24:03.260 --> 00:24:05.780
We got along just fabulously, didn't we, Ward?

00:24:05.980 --> 00:24:07.900
Oh, just like four peas in a pod.

00:24:09.110 --> 00:24:11.270
Uh, well, Aunt Martha, you don't want to miss your plane.

00:24:11.350 --> 00:24:13.110
Boys are going with you, you know. I'll call them.

00:24:15.060 --> 00:24:15.940
Wally! Beaver!

00:24:16.240 --> 00:24:18.260
They're two fine boys, June.

00:24:18.680 --> 00:24:21.740
I'm looking forward to seeing them when they come east to school.

00:24:26.560 --> 00:24:26.840
Yes.

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:31.280
I'm going to take these out the cart.

00:24:31.480 --> 00:24:32.140
OK, Dad.

00:24:34.220 --> 00:24:34.580
Beaver!

00:24:41.370 --> 00:24:42.450
Beaver, what's the idea?

00:24:42.570 --> 00:24:43.870
I thought you hated those things.

00:24:44.640 --> 00:24:45.360
I do, Dad.

00:24:45.730 --> 00:24:47.550
But I didn't want to hurt Aunt Martha's feelings.
